[32m+[m[32m# Digit Recognition using MNIST Dataset[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mA Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) that classifies handwritten digits (0-9)[m
[32m+[m[32mfrom the MNIST dataset, plus a real-world inference script that can read[m
[32m+[m[32mdigits from your own photos.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## Overview[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m- **Dataset**: MNIST (70,000 handwritten digit images, 28x28 grayscale)[m
[32m+[m[32m- **Model**: CNN (Conv2D + BatchNorm + MaxPooling + Dropout, twice, then Dense layers)[m
[32m+[m[32m- **Test Accuracy**: 99.37%[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## Project Structure[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m```[m
[32m+[m[32m├── mnist_digit_recognition.py   # Trains the CNN, evaluates it, saves plots + model[m
[32m+[m[32m├── predict_digit.py             # Predicts the digit in any photo you provide[m
[32m+[m[32m├── requirements.txt             # Python dependencies[m
[32m+[m[32m└── outputs/                     # Generated after training (model, charts, report)[m
[32m+[m[32m```[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## Setup[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m```bash[m
[32m+[m[32mpip install -r requirements.txt[m
[32m+[m[32m```[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## Usage[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m**1. Train the model:**[m
[32m+[m[32m```bash[m
[32m+[m[32mpython mnist_digit_recognition.py[m
[32m+[m[32m```[m
[32m+[m[32mDownloads the MNIST dataset, trains the CNN (~10-20 min on CPU), and saves[m
[32m+[m[32meverything to `outputs/`: the trained model, training curves, confusion[m
[32m+[m[32mmatrix, sample predictions, and a classification report.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m**2. Predict a digit from your own photo:**[m
[32m+[m[32m```bash[m
[32m+[m[32mpython predict_digit.py your_digit_photo.png[m
[32m+[m[32m```[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## A Real-World Problem I Solved[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mFeeding an ordinary phone photo straight into the model gave poor,[m
[32m+[m[32mlow-confidence predictions — even though the model itself was 99.37%[m
[32m+[m[32maccurate on the MNIST test set. The reason: MNIST digits are tiny,[m
[32m+[m[32mtightly-cropped, bold white strokes on a pure black background, while a[m
[32m+[m[32mreal photo has shadows, uneven lighting, background clutter, and thin,[m
[32m+[m[32mdim pen strokes.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m`predict_digit.py` fixes this with a preprocessing pipeline that:[m
[32m+[m[32m1. Removes shadows/uneven lighting via local background subtraction[m
[32m+[m[32m   (instead of a single global brightness threshold)[m
[32m+[m[32m2. Isolates the digit as the largest connected dark region[m
[32m+[m[32m3. Thickens the strokes to match MNIST's bold pen weight[m
[32m+[m[32m4. Stretches contrast so strokes are pure white, matching the training data[m
[32m+[m[32m5. Centers the digit on a 28x28 canvas the same way MNIST itself does[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThis took a photo that was misclassified with 45% confidence up to a[m
[32m+[m[32mcorrect prediction at 97%+ confidence.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## Model Architecture[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m```[m
[32m+[m[32mConv2D(32) -> BatchNorm -> Conv2D(32) -> MaxPool -> Dropout[m
[32m+[m[32mConv2D(64) -> BatchNorm -> Conv2D(64) -> MaxPool -> Dropout[m
[32m+[m[32mFlatten -> Dense(256) -> BatchNorm -> Dropout -> Dense(10, softmax)[m
[32m+[m[32m```[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mTrained with Adam optimizer, early stopping, and learning-rate reduction[m
[32m+[m[32mon plateau.[m
